INT. HOLLOWAY VETERINARY CLINIC - NIGHT

Rain on the tin roof. One lamp burning.

ANGLE ON the front desk. A cracked leather saddle bag sits on it, open.

CAL BRANNIGAN, 50s, sun-cured and slow, stands over a sleeping hound on the steel exam table. He strokes its ear with two rough fingers.

CAL
There. Easy, old man.

He hums. Something half-remembered.

The back door CREAKS. He doesn't turn.

COLE (O.S.)
Still got that dog's leg looked at?

CAL
Sutured an hour ago.

COLE (O.S.)
Good.

CAL
Coffee's on the stove, Cole.

COLE CALLOWAY, 40s, enters from the dark of the kennel hall. Duster coat. Dripping. He smiles like a man at a funeral that went well.

COLE
You're a hard man to find.

CAL
Not hard. Just ain't been looking.

Cole sets his hat on the counter. Takes his time.

CAL (CONT'D)
Sit.

COLE
I'll stand.

CAL
Suit yourself.

He pours two tin cups. Slides one across. Cole doesn't take it.

CLOSE ON the cup, steam curling.

COLE
Remember when we pulled that mare out of the Platte?

CAL
Spring of '09.

COLE
She had a foal in her.

CAL
Dead already.

COLE
You cried anyway.

Cal almost laughs. He looks at the hound instead.

CAL
I was young.

COLE
You were thirty-eight.

A beat. The rain softens. Nothing but the clock.

COLE (CONT'D)
Nice quiet place you've made.

CAL
It does the job.

COLE
Folks bring you strays? Hurt things?

CAL
Mostly.

COLE
And you fix them.

CAL
When I can.

Cole nods. He walks the length of the counter, one hand trailing along it. He stops at the saddle bag.

INSERT - THE BAG

A leather pouch inside. Tied with red twine.

BACK TO SCENE

Cole doesn't touch it. He just looks.

COLE
Heavier than it ought to be.

CAL
Feed money.

COLE
Is that right.

CAL
That's right.

Cole smiles. Cal does not.

A phone RINGS on the wall. Old black rotary. Neither man moves. It rings five times, six.

CAL (CONT'D)
Let it ring.

COLE
Could be somebody's sick animal.

CAL
Could be.

He lifts the receiver anyway. Holds it between them so both can hear.

MARSHAL TUCKER (V.O.)
(tinny, through the line)
Cal? That you? Got a rider heading your way. Cole Calloway. Armed, and he ain't alone.

Silence. Cole's smile hasn't moved.

CAL
(into the phone)
He's here.

MARSHAL TUCKER (V.O.)
Cal, get out of there.

Cal hangs up. Gently.

COLE
You knew.

CAL
Saw your horse. Tied behind the feed store.

COLE
And you made coffee.

CAL
Seemed polite.

WE SEE Cole's hand drift to his coat. Slow.

ANGLE ON Cal's other hand, under the counter.

CAL (CONT'D)
That hound on the table.

COLE
What of him?

CAL
You shot him. Day before yesterday. Outside Dunmore.

Cole's hand stops.

CAL (CONT'D)
Took me three hours to dig the lead out.

COLE
(quiet)
He was on my trail.

CAL
He was a dog.

The hound stirs. Lifts his head. Looks at Cole.

A low growl, half asleep.

COLE
Hell.

CAL
Said you'd come for him.

COLE
Who said?

CAL
Nobody. He just kept looking at the door.

Cole laughs, short. Then he doesn't.

COLE
What's under that counter, Cal?

CAL
Same thing that was under it in '09.

Rain. Nothing else.

Cole lets his hand fall away from the coat.

COLE
I'll be damned.

CAL
Likely.

Cole picks up his hat. Puts it on. Turns for the door.

CAL (CONT'D)
Cole.

He stops.

CAL (CONT'D)
Leave the pouch.

COLE
It ain't yours.

CAL
Not asking for me.

Cole looks at the dog. Then at the bag. He lays the twine-tied pouch on the counter, gentle as a newborn.

He goes out into the rain.

Cal lets out a long breath. Brings his hand out from under the counter.

It's holding a tin of dog biscuits.

He gives the hound one.
